# tRPC Procedure Refactoring Guidelines

This document outlines best practices for refactoring tRPC procedures that contain Supabase queries into more structured domain-specific modules.

## Current Pattern Issues

Currently, many tRPC procedures directly contain Supabase queries and business logic:

```typescript
// In src/server/api/routers/sessions.ts
getSessionById: protectedProcedure
  .input(z.object({ sessionId: z.string() }))
  .query(async ({ input }) => {
    const supabase = await createClient();

    const { data, error } = await supabase
      .from("sessions")
      .select(
        `
          *,
          user:profiles!user_id(*),
          coach:profiles!coach_id(*),
          event:calendar_events_v2!event_id(*)
        `,
      )
      .eq("id", input.sessionId)
      .single();

    if (error) {
      throw new TRPCError({
        code: "NOT_FOUND",
        message: "Session not found",
        cause: error,
      });
    }

    return data;
  }),
```

This pattern leads to:

- Procedures becoming complex and hard to test
- Business logic mixed with data access code
- Duplication across different procedures
- Difficult to reuse logic outside of tRPC context

## Recommended Refactoring Pattern

Refactor tRPC procedures by moving Supabase queries and business logic to domain-specific service modules in the `lib` folder.

### Existing Good Examples

The project already has some procedures that follow this pattern:

```typescript
// In src/server/api/routers/sessions.ts
getAllSessionsForUser: publicProcedure
  .input(
    z.object({
      profileId: z.string(),
      eventType: z.enum(["coaching_session", "meeting"]).optional(),
    }),
  )
  .query(async ({ input }) => {
    return handleGetAllSessionsForUser(input, await createClient());
  }),
```

The logic has been moved to a domain-specific module:

```typescript
// In src/lib/sessions/queries.ts
export async function handleGetAllSessionsForUser(
  input: GetAllSessionsForUserInput,
  supabase: SupabaseClient<Database>
) {
  const { profileId } = input;

  // Database query and business logic implementation
  // ...

  return finalSessions;
}
```

### Complete Refactoring Framework

#### 1. Create Domain-Specific Service Modules

Create a service module for each domain area in the `lib` folder:

```
src/
  lib/
    domain-name/
      queries.ts       # Database queries
      services.ts      # Core service functions
      transformers.ts  # Data transformation logic
      schemas.ts       # Domain-specific Zod schemas
      validators.ts    # Validation logic
      index.ts         # Public API
```

#### 2. Define Input/Output Schemas

Create explicit input and output schemas for your functions:

```typescript
// src/lib/domain-name/schemas.ts
import { z } from "zod";

export const EntityRetrieveInput = z.object({
  id: z.string(),
});
export type EntityRetrieveInput = z.infer<typeof EntityRetrieveInput>;

export const EntityRetrieveOutput = z.object({
  id: z.string(),
  name: z.string(),
  // ... other fields
});
export type EntityRetrieveOutput = z.infer<typeof EntityRetrieveOutput>;
```

#### 3. Move Database Queries to Query Modules

Extract all Supabase queries into dedicated query modules:

```typescript
// src/lib/domain-name/queries.ts
import { TRPCError } from "@trpc/server";
import type { EntityRetrieveInput, EntityRetrieveOutput } from "./schemas";
import type { SupabaseClient<Database>  } from "~/lib/supabase/server";

export async function getEntityById(
  input: EntityRetrieveInput,
  supabase: SupabaseClient<Database>
) {
  const { id } = input;

  const { data, error } = await supabase
    .from("entity_table")
    .select("*")
    .eq("id", id)
    .single();

  if (error) {
    throw new TRPCError({
      code: "NOT_FOUND",
      message: "Entity not found",
      cause: error,
    });
  }

  return data satisfies EntityRetrieveOutput;
}
```

#### 4. Create Service Functions for Business Logic

Create service functions that use the query modules:

```typescript
// src/lib/domain-name/services.ts
import type { EntityRetrieveInput, EntityRetrieveOutput } from "./schemas";
import * as queries from "./queries";
import type { SupabaseClient<Database>  } from "~/lib/supabase/server";

export async function getEntity(
  input: EntityRetrieveInput,
  supabase: SupabaseClient<Database>
): Promise<EntityRetrieveOutput> {
  const rawData = await queries.getEntityById(input, supabase);

  // Perform any additional business logic, transformations, etc.

  return {
    id: rawData.id,
    name: rawData.name,
    // Transform other fields as needed
  };
}
```

#### 5. Simplify tRPC Procedures

Use the service functions in your tRPC procedures:

```typescript
// src/server/api/routers/domain-name.ts
import { createTRPCRouter, protectedProcedure } from "../trpc";
import { EntityRetrieveInput } from "~/lib/domain-name/schemas";
import * as domainService from "~/lib/domain-name/services";
import { createClient } from "~/lib/supabase/server";

export const domainRouter = createTRPCRouter({
  getEntity: protectedProcedure
    .input(EntityRetrieveInput)
    .query(async ({ input }) => {
      return domainService.getEntity(input, await createClient());
    }),
});
```

